    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#scenarios" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The Four Scenarios</a>
          <a href="#easy-case" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The Easy Case</a>
          <a href="#darker" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Going Darker or More Opaque</a>
          <a href="#lighter" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Going Lighter Does Not Work</a>
          <a href="#carrier" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Switching Oil and Water</a>
          <a href="#test" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. How to Test Properly</a>
          <a href="#failed" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. What a Failed Recoat Looks Like</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The reason this question matters so much is that the alternative is stripping, which is the single most unpleasant job in deck maintenance. Getting the answer right saves a weekend; getting it wrong costs two.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            You can stain over old stain when three conditions hold: the old finish has worn enough that water soaks into the wood, the new product is compatible with the old one, and you are going the same colour or darker. Fail any of the three and the recoat will not work.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Test That Answers It</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Sprinkle water on the deck in several places. Soaks in everywhere: recoat. Beads anywhere: that area needs stripping.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A working water repellent is doing exactly what it was bought to do, which includes repelling the carrier in your new stain. Beading is not a sign the old finish is good; it is a sign the deck is not ready for a new one.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Test in at least five places, including a walkway, a sheltered corner, under where furniture sits, and the sunniest boards. Mixed results are the most important finding, and they are covered in section eight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="scenarios" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Four Scenarios
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What You Have</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What You Want</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Verdict</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Worn semi-transparent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Same product, same colour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean, brighten, recoat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Worn semi-transparent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Darker or more opaque</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Works well; hides unevenness</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Any stain still beading</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Anything</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip, or wait for it to weather</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solid stain, sound film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solid stain again</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean, scrape loose areas, recoat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solid stain or paint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Semi-transparent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full strip required</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Oil-based, still present</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water-based</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip first; poor adhesion otherwise</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Peeling film of any kind</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Anything</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove it; a recoat peels with it</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two of these rows are the ones that account for most disappointment. Applying anything over a deck that still beads water is the most common, and trying to go from solid back to semi-transparent is the most expensive.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="easy-case" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Easy Case, and It Is the Common One
          </h2>

          <p>
            A deck finished two or three years ago with a semi-transparent penetrating stain, now faded and letting water in, recoated with the same product in the same colour, is the standard maintenance cycle and it is genuinely easy. Wash it with a deck cleaner, apply a brightener, let it dry properly, and apply one fresh coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            No stripping, no sanding, no primer. This is exactly the advantage penetrating stains hold over film finishes, and it is why they are the default recommendation for most decks. The whole job is a Saturday of cleaning and a Sunday or Monday of staining once the wood has dried.
          </p>

          <p>
            The only judgement required is whether the wear is even. If the deck has faded uniformly, recoat and you are done. If the walkway is bare while the perimeter still repels water, you have the mixed case rather than the easy one, and that changes the answer. The full preparation routine is in <a href="/how-to-prep-a-deck-for-staining/">prepping a deck for staining</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="darker" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Going Darker or More Opaque
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the direction that works, and it works better than staying the same in one important respect: it hides unevenness. Extra pigment covers the variation left behind by uneven wear, so a deck that would look patchy under a repeat of its original colour can look uniform under something a shade darker.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moving up the opacity ladder does the same thing more emphatically. A deck whose semi-transparent finish has worn badly is a natural candidate for a semi-solid or solid coat, which will cover the history entirely. This is the normal life cycle of a deck: transparent early, more opaque as it ages.
          </p>

          <p>
            The caution is that each step up is easier to take than to reverse. Going solid buys years and coverage but commits you to solid, because a penetrating stain cannot get through an opaque film later. Take the step when the deck gives you a reason, not just because it is available — the trade-off is laid out in <a href="/solid-vs-semi-transparent-deck-stain/">solid versus semi-transparent stain</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lighter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Going Lighter Does Not Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stain adds colour. It cannot remove it. A lighter product applied over a darker existing finish simply disappears into it, and the deck stays the colour it already was — possibly slightly muddier. There is no version of this that works by application alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            The only route to a lighter deck is removal: stripping the existing finish, then sanding if the wood itself has darkened, then applying the new colour to bare timber. On a deck that has carried a dark stain for years the wood underneath may be permanently altered, so even a full strip may not deliver the pale result you have in mind.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before committing to that, strip a test area of about a square foot in a visible location and look at what you get. That tells you whether the lighter colour is achievable at all, and it is a far cheaper way to find out than stripping an entire deck first. The process is covered in <a href="/how-to-strip-deck-stain/">how to strip deck stain</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="carrier" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Switching Between Oil and Water-Based
          </h2>

          <p>
            Applying a water-based stain over an oil-based finish that is still present is unreliable and frequently fails, because acrylic resin does not bond well to an oily surface. The result is a coat that looks fine initially and lifts within a season. Oil over water-based is somewhat more forgiving but still not recommended by most manufacturers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The important nuance is what "still present" means. If the old oil stain has genuinely worn away — the deck is thirsty, water soaks in everywhere, and there is no film left — then the wood is effectively bare and either type can go on. The problem is only with an old finish that is still doing its job.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice this makes the first choice a long-term one. Choosing oil sets a default toward oil recoats; choosing water-based does the same in reverse. It is not an absolute lock, but changing direction usually means either stripping or waiting an extra season for the old finish to wear off entirely. The trade-offs between the two are covered in <a href="/oil-based-vs-water-based-deck-stain/">oil versus water-based deck stain</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Test Properly Before Committing
          </h2>

          <p>
            The water test tells you whether the deck can absorb anything. A test patch tells you whether this specific product will behave on this specific deck, and it is worth doing whenever you are changing brand, type, colour or opacity.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Prepare the test area exactly as you will prepare the deck</strong> — same cleaner, same brightener, same drying time. A patch on unprepared wood tells you nothing useful.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Choose somewhere representative but discreet</strong> — under a step, at the edge of an area furniture covers, or on a board in the shade rather than the sun.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Watch how fast it absorbs.</strong> Gone in a few minutes means the deck is ready. Still glossy after fifteen means it is not.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Leave it at least a day or two.</strong> Tackiness, dull patches and colour shifts all show up after the fact rather than immediately.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Check the colour in daylight</strong>, not under a porch light or in the store. Stain looks different on aged wood than on the sample card.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            A test patch costs a quart of stain and two days of waiting. Discovering the same information across an entire deck costs a stripping weekend, so the arithmetic is not close.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="failed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What a Failed Recoat Looks Like
          </h2>

          <p>
            The symptoms appear on a predictable timeline. Within days, the deck looks blotchy, with darker patches where the stain absorbed and lighter, shinier patches where it did not. Areas may be tacky underfoot and pick up dust and footprints.
          </p>

          <p>
            Within weeks, the unabsorbed stain begins to wear off in exactly those shiny areas, usually starting where people walk, so the deck develops light paths across a darker field. Within a season, the surplus that remains can start lifting in flakes, which is a film failure rather than the graceful thinning a well-applied penetrating stain shows.
          </p>

          <p>
            The mixed-absorption deck is the most common cause. A deck where the walkway has worn bare while the sheltered edges still repel water will absorb stain at two rates and dry two colours, and no amount of care during application prevents it. That deck needed stripping — or at least stripping of the areas still repelling — before anything new went on, and it is the case most often missed because those areas look perfectly fine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Recoating a Solid-Stained Deck</h2>
        <p>
          Solid stain follows different rules because it is a film rather than a penetrating finish. Where the film is sound and well adhered, recoating with another solid stain is straightforward maintenance: wash the deck, scrape and sand any loose or lifting areas, feather the edges so they do not telegraph through, spot-prime bare wood, and apply the new coat.
        </p>
        <p>
          The limit is film thickness. Each coat adds to it, and a coating that has been recoated several times becomes too rigid to move with the wood through its seasonal expansion and starts cracking. If a solid-stained deck is on its third or fourth coat and beginning to craze, the accumulated thickness is likely the cause, and adding another layer makes it worse rather than better.
        </p>
        <p>
          Test adhesion before committing. Press a strip of strong tape firmly onto the coating in a few places and pull it off sharply. If the film comes away with the tape, it is no longer bonded and a recoat will fail with it. If it stays put, the film is sound and another coat is reasonable.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">The Patience Option</h2>
        <p>
          There is a third path between recoating and stripping that people rarely consider: waiting. If the deck still beads water in places, the old finish is still protecting the wood, and there is no urgency to do anything at all. Another season of weathering will wear those areas down to match the rest, and next year the whole deck may pass the water test uniformly.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is a legitimate answer, particularly when the alternative is stripping. A deck whose finish is unevenly worn but still working is not in danger; it is simply not ready for its next coat. Letting it even out costs nothing but a year of slightly patchy appearance, and it converts a stripping job into a normal recoat.
        </p>
        <p>
          The judgement is whether the bare areas are exposed enough to start greying and eroding while you wait. If the worn paths are still beading a little, or the deck is shaded, waiting is safe. If bare wood is going grey and rough in the traffic areas, the clock is running and stripping the still-sealed areas is the better call. Our guide to <a href="/how-often-to-stain-a-deck/">how often to stain a deck</a> covers reading those signs.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">A Practical Decision Sequence</h2>
        <p>
          Work through it in this order and the answer usually becomes obvious. First, is anything peeling or flaking? If so, it has to come off, whatever else is true. Second, does water soak in everywhere? If it beads anywhere, either strip those areas or wait for them to weather. Third, are you going lighter or more transparent? If so, stripping is the only route.
        </p>
        <p>
          Fourth, are you switching between oil and water-based while the old finish is still present? If so, strip. Fifth, if none of those apply, clean the deck, brighten it, let it dry properly, run a test patch, and recoat. That is the great majority of decks in any given year, and it is a genuinely easy job.
        </p>
        <p>
          Doing that sequence honestly is the whole skill here. The temptation is always to conclude that the deck is fine to recoat because the alternative is unpleasant, and the deck is indifferent to that reasoning. Ten minutes with a cup of water gives you the real answer, and it is right far more often than optimism is.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating a Deck That Still Beads Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A working repellent stops the new stain penetrating, so it sits on the surface, dries blotchy and wears off in patches within a season. Beading is not a sign the deck is in good shape for a recoat; it is a sign it is not ready for one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing in Only One Spot</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Decks wear unevenly, and the walkway often fails a year before the sheltered edges. A single test tells you about that spot only. Mixed absorption is the most common cause of a blotchy recoat, and it is invisible until the stain goes on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Lighter Colour From a New Coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stain adds colour and cannot remove it, so a lighter product over a darker finish simply disappears into it. The only route to a lighter deck is stripping, and on wood that has carried dark stain for years even that may not deliver the result you pictured.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Switching Carrier Type Over an Active Finish</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water-based stain does not bond reliably to an oily surface, so it looks fine at first and lifts within a season. If the old oil finish has genuinely worn away and water soaks in everywhere, either type can go on; otherwise strip first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding Yet Another Coat of Solid Stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each coat adds film thickness, and a film that has been built up over several cycles becomes too rigid to flex with the wood and starts cracking. If a solid-stained deck is crazing, another layer accelerates the problem instead of covering it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you stain over old deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, when the old stain has worn enough that water soaks into the wood, the product is compatible, and you are going the same colour or darker. If water beads anywhere, the finish is a peeling film, or you want a lighter result, strip first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you have to remove old stain before restaining a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not usually with penetrating stains, which is their main advantage. A faded semi-transparent stain that lets water in can be cleaned, brightened and recoated. Removal is required for peeling films, for switching carrier types, and for going lighter or more transparent.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you know if you can stain over an old stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sprinkle water on the deck in several places. Soaking in and darkening the wood everywhere within a minute or two means a new coat can penetrate. Beading anywhere means that area still has an active finish and a new coat will sit on top and fail.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a darker stain over a lighter one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is the easiest recoat there is. Going darker or more opaque works because the new pigment covers the old, so unevenness underneath is hidden rather than highlighted. Going lighter does not work, because stain adds colour and cannot remove it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a different brand of stain over an old one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, provided the carrier type matches and the old finish has worn enough to accept the new one. Brand matters far less than whether both are oil or both water-based, and whether the wood can actually absorb what you apply. Test a small area regardless.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if you stain over stain that has not worn off?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The new coat cannot penetrate, so it sits on the surface, dries unevenly and often stays tacky. Within a season it wears off in patches or lifts, leaving a deck that looks worse than before. The water test exists precisely to prevent this.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you stain over a solid stain without stripping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can apply another solid stain over a sound existing solid coat after cleaning and scraping anything loose, and that is normal maintenance. What you cannot do is apply a semi-transparent stain over it, because a penetrating product cannot get through an opaque film.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you test whether a new stain is compatible?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Prepare a small area exactly as you will prepare the whole deck, apply the new stain, and leave it a day or two. Check that it absorbed rather than sitting on the surface, that it is not tacky, and that the colour is what you expected before committing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

