        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            You can recoat when three things are true: water soaks into the wood everywhere, the new product matches the old one's carrier type, and you are going the same colour or darker. That describes most decks most years, and it is why penetrating stains are the low-maintenance choice.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Stripping is required for peeling films, for moving from solid back to semi-transparent, for switching between oil and water-based while the old finish is still active, and for going lighter. Nothing about careful application substitutes for removal in those cases.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Test in five places, not one — mixed absorption across a deck is the most common cause of a blotchy recoat and the easiest thing to miss. And remember the third option: if parts of the deck still bead water, waiting a season for them to even out turns a stripping job into a normal recoat.
          </p>
        </div>


